row}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{0}_{\\large{ { {\\color{blue}1}} \\text{ zero}}}\\!\\!\\!$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n6\\begin{vmatrix}. & 0 \\\\ + & 1\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid. \\mid 4;\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1; 6\\begin{vmatrix}. & 0 \\\\ + & 1\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}. & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6 + 16.4 = 22.4\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186138157 "There are no decimal places in $56$, and there are $\\color{blue}4$ decimal places in $10.3691$.\n\nTo add the two numbers, we need them to have the same number of decimal places. So, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}4$ decimal places:\n$56\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\underbrace{0000}_{\\large{ { {\\color{blue}4}} \\text{ zeros}}}\\!$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}6 &. & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 1 & 0 &. & 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}9 \\mid 1;\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}6 &. & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 1 & 0 &. & 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}9\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}. & 3 & 6 & 9\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n56 + 10.3691 = 66.3691\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186138174 "In this question, $\\color{blue}8$ is multiplied by itself $\\color{red}4$ times:\n$\\underbrace{ {\\color{blue}8} \\times {\\color{blue}8} \\times {\\color{blue}8} \\times {\\color{blue}8}}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}4}} \\text{ times}}}$\n\nSo, in exponent form, the base must be $\\color{blue}8$ and the exponent must be ${\\color{red}4}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\times {\\color{blue}8} \\times {\\color{blue}8} \\times {\\color{blue}8} = {\\color{blue}8}^{\\color{red}4}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186138175 "In this question, $\\color{blue}18$ is multiplied by itself $\\color{red}3$ times:\n$\\underbrace{ {\\color{blue}18} \\times {\\color{blue}18} \\times {\\color{blue}18}}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}3}} \\text{ times}}}.$\n\nSo, in exponent form, the base must be $\\color{blue}18$ and the exponent must be ${\\color{red}3}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{\\color{blue}18} \\times {\\color{blue}18} \\times {\\color{blue}18}}= {\\color{blue}18}^{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that ${\\color{blue}18}^{\\color{red}3}$ means that the base is ${\\color{blue}18}$, **not** $8$." #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186138176 "Here, ${\\color{blue}6}^{\\color{red}5}$ is in exponent form. The base is $\\color{blue}6$ and the exponent is ${\\color{red}5}$.\n\nThis means that $\\color{blue}6$ is multiplied by itself $\\color{red}5$ times:\n$\\underbrace{ {\\color{blue}6} \\times {\\color{blue}6} \\times {\\color{blue}6} \\times {\\color{blue}6} \\times {\\color{blue}6}}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}5}} \\text{ times}}}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6^5 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186138177 "We notice that ${\\color{blue}234}^{\\color{red}4}$ is in exponent form.\n\nFirst, note that the base is **NOT** $4$. The base is **NOT** $34$ either.\n\nInstead, the base is the full number ${\\color{blue}234}$, and the exponent is ${\\color{red}4}$. This means that $\\color{blue}234$ is multiplied by itself $\\color{red}4$ times:\n$\\underbrace{ {\\color{blue}234} \\times {\\color{blue}234} \\times {\\color{blue}234} \\times {\\color{blue}234} }_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}4}} \\text{ times}}}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n234^4 = 234 \\times 234 \\times 234 \\times 234\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186138178 "First, we can expand $10^1$ by writing a $1$ followed by a block of $1$ zero:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{29} \\times 10^{\\color{red}1} = \\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{29} \\times \\!\\!\\underbrace{\\!1\\:\\!\\color{blue}0}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}1}} \\text{ zero}}}\\!\\!\\!\n$$\n\nNow, to find $29 \\times 10$, we join $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{29}$ with the block of zeros:\n$\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{29} \\times \\!\\!\\underbrace{\\!1\\:\\!\\color{blue}0}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}1}} \\text{ zero}}}\\!\\!\\! = \\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{29}{\\color{blue}0}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n29 \\times 10^1 = 290\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186138179 "To calculate the total number of sheets of paper, we need to multiply $7$ by $10^2$.\n\nFirst, we notice that\n$10^{\\color{red}2} = 1\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{\\color{blue}00}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}2}} \\text{ zeros}}}\\!\\!\\!.$\n\nSo, we have\n$\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{7} \\times 10^{\\color{red}2} = \\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{7} \\times 1\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{\\color{blue}00}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}2}} \\text{ zeros}}}\\!\\!\\!.$\n\nNow, to find $7 \\times 100$, we join $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{7}$ with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{7} \\times 1\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{\\color{blue}00}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}2}} \\text{ zeros}}}\\!\\!\\! = \\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{7}{\\color{blue}00}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7 \\times 10^2 = 700\n$$\n\nWe conclude that Tom bought $700$ sheets of paper." #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186138180 "First, we can expand out $10^3$ by writing a $1$ followed by a block of $3$ zeros:\n\n$$\n10^{\\color{red}3} = 1\\!\\!\\underbrace{\\color{blue}000}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}3}} \\text{ zeros}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we have:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{173} \\times 10^{\\color{red}3} = \\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{173} \\times 1\\!\\!\\underbrace{\\color{blue}000}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}3}} \\text{ zeros}}}\n$$\n\nNow, to find $173 \\times 1,000$, we join $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{173}$ with the block of zeros:\n$\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{17