ext{ digits}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(30.1)^2 = 9.0601\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186147187 "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4$ with multiplication signs:\n$\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^{\\color{blue}{4}} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8}}_{\\large { { {\\color{blue}4}} \\text{ times}}}$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{3}{8})^{4}|= \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8}; = \\frac{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}{8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8}; = (3 \\cdot 3^(⏞)^(9) \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3^(⏞)^(9))/(8 \\cdot 8_(⏟)_(64) \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8_(⏟)_(64)); = \\frac{9 \\cdot 9}{64 \\cdot 64}; = \\frac{81}{4096}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{81}{4\\,096}}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186147192 "Let's compute the interquartile range for the number of coconut ice creams sold.\n\nNotice that our data set is already arranged from smallest to greatest, and that\nthe number of data points is even ($8$).\n\n- The lower part is the first half of the data:\n$\\underbrace{22, \\: 35, \\: 43, \\: 52,}_{\\text{lower half}} \\: {58, \\: 65, \\: 68, \\:72}$\nNow, we find the median of the lower part. In this case, it's the mean of the two middle numbers:\n$22, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{35}}, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{43}}, \\: 52$\nSo, the lower quartile is\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{red}35}+{\\color{red}43}}{2}=\\dfrac{78}{2}=39$.\n\n- The upper part is the second half of the data:\n${22, \\: 35, \\: 43, \\: 52,} \\: \\underbrace{58, \\: 65, \\: 68, \\:72}_{\\text{upper half}}$\nNow, we find the median of the upper part. In this case, it's the mean of the two middle numbers:\n$58, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{65}}, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{68}}, \\: 72$\nSo, the upper quartile is\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{red}65}+{\\color{red}68}}{2}=\\dfrac{133}{2}=66.5$.\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range of the data set is\n\n$$\n\\text{upper quartile} - \\text{lower quartile} = 66.5 - 39= 27.5\n$$\n\nSo, the interquartile range of the number of coconut ice creams sold per day is $27.5$\n\nLet's now examine the statements one by one:\n\n- Statement I is true.\n- Statement II is true. Since $30 > 27.5$, the interquartile range is larger for the strawberry ice creams\n- Statement III is true. The IQR is greater for strawberry ice creams, indicating more variability in the data than for coconut ice creams, suggesting that it's more difficult for Tom to predict the number of strawberry ice creams he'll sell tomorrow.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 13194139533366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186147259 "First, we calculate $4^2$. We have\n$4^{\\color{blue}2} = \\underbrace{4 \\times 4}_{\\large { { {\\color{blue}2}} \\text{ times}}} = 16.$\n\nThen, for $1^7$, we have\n\n$$\n1^{7} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{red}7} [/math] times) = 1\n$$\n\nBecause $16 > 1$, the correct statement must be\n$4^2 > 1^7 \\,$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186147261 "**Watch Out!** Remember that,\n\n- $-2^3$ is shorthand for $-\\!\\left(2^3\\right)$,\n- similarly, $-3^2$ is shorthand for $-\\!\\left(3^2\\right)$.\n\nFirst, we calculate $-2^3$. We ignore the negative sign and calculate the positive exponent first:\n$2^{\\color{blue}3}  = \\underbrace{2 \\times 2 \\times 2}_{\\large { { {\\color{blue}3}} \\text{ times}}} = 8$\n\nTherefore, $-2^3 = -8$.\n\nNow, to compute $-3^2$, we again ignore the negative sign and calculate the positive exponent first:\n\n$$\n3^{2} = 3 \\cdot 3_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{red}2} [/math] times) = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-3^2 = -9$.\n\nFinally, let's look at the number line to compare $-8$ and $-9$.\n\n![](images/52/529d74b465ca32e111aac2a138222228b1aba1c06d7baf92bf8c5a84b3d78e09.png)\n\nSince $-8 > -9$, the correct statement must be\n$-2^3 > -3^2 \\,$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186147326 "Notice that our data set is already arranged from smallest to greatest, where we have\n\n$$\nsmallest value = 4, greatest value = 15\n$$\n\nSince the number of data points is odd ($7$), the median of the data set is the middle number. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{median} = \\color{blue}12\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the quartiles by considering the lower and upper halves of the distribution:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{4, \\: 5, \\: 6,}_{\\text{lower half}} \\: {\\color{blue}12}, \\: \\underbrace{13, \\: 13, \\: 15}_{\\text{upper half}}\n$$\n\nThe lower quartile is the median of the lower half. In this case, it's the middle number:\n\n$$\n\\text{lower quartile} = 5\n$$\n\nThe upper quartile is the median of the upper half. In this case, it's the middle number:\n\n$$\n\\text{upper quartile} = 13\n$$\n\nFinally, we draw the corresponding boxplot, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bd69426498e43ecc4bdd4fa4f7954451fface95bc37a87abc16729fa58c85f46.png)\n\nTherefore, the boxplot representing this data is diagram $\\textrm I$." #edb/ref 13194139533366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186147336 "Here, $F(h)$ is a density function for the number of dust particles in the air, and its units are millions of particles per $1 \\, \\text{ft}^3$.\n\nThe mass of a single vertical column measuring\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2}_{\\textrm{cross section}} \\times \\:\\: \\underbrace{100\\, \\textrm{ft}}_{\\textrm{height}}\n$$\n\nis given by the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{100} F(h) \\, \\text{d}h\n$$\n\nHowever, since we want the mass of $5$ vertical columns (since that will give a cross-section of $5\\,\\textrm{ft}^2)$, we need to multiply the above integral by $5$. So the required mass is given by\n\n$$\n5 \\int_{0}^{100} F(h) \\, \\text{d}h\n$$\n\nCarrying out the integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= 5\\int_{0}^{100}40e^{-0.5d}dh \\\\\n&= 200\\int_{0}^{100}e^{-0.5d}dh \\\\\n&= \\left.200[- \\frac{1}{0.5} \\cdot e^{-0.5d}]\\right|_{0}^{100} \\\\\n&= \\left.- 400e^{-0.5d}\\right|_{0}^{100} \\\\\n&=-400(e^{-50} - 1) \\\\\n&= 400(1 - e^{-50})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the column contains\n\n$$\n400 \\left(1-e^{-50} \\right)\n$$\n\nmillion particles.\n\n**Note:** We can say that the number of particles is around $400$ million since the number $e^{-50}$ inside the parenthesis is extremely small (it's almost zero)." #edb/ref 13194139533366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186147337 "Here, the function $c(h)$ is a density function for the mass of the substance, and its units are $\\text{mg}/\\text{m}^3$.\n\nThe mass of a single vertical column measuring\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{1\\,\\textrm{m}^2}_{\\textrm{cross section}} \\times \\:\\: \\underbrace{30\\, \\textrm{m}}_{\\textrm{height}}\n$$\n\nis given by the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{30} c(h) \\, \\text{d}h\n$$\n\nHowever, since we want the mass of $4$ vertical columns (since that will give a cross-section of $4\\,\\textrm{m}^2)$, we need to multiply the above integral by $4$. So the required mass is given by\n\n$$\nM = 4 \\int_{0}^{30} c(h) \\, \\text{d}h\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= 4\\int_{0}^{30}c(h)dh \\\\\n&= 4(\\int_{0}^{20}c(h)dh + \\int_{20}^{30}c(h)dh) \\\\\n&= 4[\\int_{0}^{20}(30 - h)dh + \\int_{20}^{30}10e^{2 - 0.1h}dh] \\\\\n&= 4\\int_{0}^{20}(30 - h)dh + 40\\int_{20}^{30}e^{2 - 0.1h}dh\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186151812 "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n\\forall\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, \\exists y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x+y=0\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ For every real $x$ there exists real $y$ such that $x+y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true since $2+y=0$ for some real number $y$ (e.g., for $y=-2$).\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(x): \\quad \\exists \\, y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x+y=0\n$$\n\nis true for any real $x.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(1): \\quad \\exists \\, y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, 1 + y=0\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $y=-1.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2): \\quad \\exists \\, y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, 2+ y=0\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $y=-2.$ And so on. In general, it's sufficient to take $y=-x.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n\\big( \\, \\forall\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, \\exists y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x+y=0 \\, \\big)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\, \\big( \\, \\underbrace{\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x+y=0}_{P(x)} \\, \\big)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(x)$ is true over the universal set $\\mathbb R.$ And, according to statement II, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 13194139533457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186151816 "Given that the implication $S \\Rightarrow N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textbf{S}\\textrm{ufficient} \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\textbf{N}\\textrm{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\big( \\, A=\\overline{B} \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{sufficient}} \n\\: \\Rightarrow \\: \n\\underbrace{\\big( \\, A\\cap B = \\emptyset \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement II is true. $A=\\overline{B}$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A\\cap B = \\emptyset.$\n\n- Statement III is true. $A\\cap B = \\emptyset$ is a **necessary** condition for $A=\\overline{B}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 13194139534415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157060 "The contrapositive of $P \\Rightarrow Q$ is\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the statement\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7 \\not\\mid a}_{P} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{7 \\not\\mid 10a}_{Q}\n$$\n\nThe contrapositive form of the statement is\n$\\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(7 \\not\\mid 10a \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, Q} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(7 \\not\\mid a \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, P}$,\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n$7 \\mid 10a \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 7 \\mid a$." #edb/ref 13194139533368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157062 "Recall that the conditional statement $P \\Rightarrow Q$ and its contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nare logically equivalent. Moreover, it's sometimes easier to prove the contrapositive.\n\nIn this case, we need to show that\n$\\underbrace{(36 \\not\\mid a)}_{P} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{(20 \\not\\mid a) \\:\\lor\\: (45 \\not\\mid a)}_{Q}$.\nThe contrapositive form of the statement is\n$\\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big((20 \\not\\mid a) \\:\\lor\\: (45 \\not\\mid a) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, Q} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(36 \\not\\mid a \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, P}$,\nwhich is equivalent to\n$(20 \\mid a) \\: \\land\\: (45 \\mid a) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 36 \\mid a$.\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> The contrapositive of the statement is\n> $(20 \\mid a) \\: \\land\\: (45 \\mid a) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 36 \\mid a$.\n> We will prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nFirst, we recall the following fact:\n$(d \\mid a) \\:\\land\\: (a \\mid b) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad d \\mid b$.\n\nTherefore, we start our proof as follows:\n\n> Because $4 \\mid 20$ and $9 \\mid 45$, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}20∣a & ⇒4∣a, \\\\ 45∣a & ⇒9∣a\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNotice that $4$ and $9$ are coprime. As a result, their least common multiple equals the product of the numbers:\n\n> Since $4$ and $9$ are coprime, their least common multiple is given by\n> $\\textrm{lcm}(4,9) = 36$.\n\nWe now make use of the division algorithm:\n\n> By the division algorithm, there exist integers $q$ and $r$ such that\n> $a = 36q + r, \\qquad 0 \\leq r \\lt 36$.\n\nWe resort to the division algorithm because it closely resembles what we want, namely $a=36q$. Our goal is to show that $r=0$.\n\nThe next step is to reason that $r$ (which we know should equal zero) is divisible by $4$ and $9$. This is easier to see if we isolate $r$, so let's do that.\n\n> Solving the left equation for $r$, we get $r =a - 36q$.\n\nSince both terms on the right-hand side are divisible by $4$ and $9$, we can reason that $r$ is divisible by $4$ and $9{:}$\n\n> We have that $4 \\mid a$ and $9 \\mid a$. Also, $36$ is divisible by both these numbers. So, $4 \\mid r$ and $9 \\mid r$. This means $r$ is a common multiple of $4$ and $9$.\n\nThe realization that $r$ is a common multiple of $4$ and $9$ is the key to unlocking the proof.\n\nTo help us visualize the candidates for $r$, let's write down a few common multiples of $4$ and $9{:}$\n$\\ldots,\\qquad -72, \\qquad -36, \\qquad 0, \\qquad 36, \\qquad 72, \\qquad \\ldots$\n\nThe *only* common multiple of $4$ and $9$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n0 \\leq r \\lt 36\n$$\n\nis zero.\n\n> However, since $36$ is the least common multiple of $4$ and $9$, and $0 \\leq r \\lt 36$, we must have $r=0$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na=36q+0=36q\n$$\n\nand we make our conclusion using the definition of integer divisibility:\n\n> Therefore, since $a=36q$, we have $36 \\mid a$." #edb/ref 13194139533368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157063 "The contrapositive of $P \\Rightarrow Q$ is\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the statement\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 9)}_{P} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{7a \\not\\equiv 7b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 9)}_{Q}\n$$\n\nThe contrapositive form of this statement is\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(7a \\not\\equiv 7b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 9) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, Q} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 9) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, P}\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7a \\equiv 7b} \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 9) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a \\equiv b} \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 9)\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157064 "Sometimes, to prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it is easier to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nthat is equivalent to the initial implication.\n\nIn this case, we need to show that\n$\\underbrace{a+7 \\not\\equiv b+7 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)}_{P} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)}_{Q}$.\nThe contrapositive of the statement is\n$\\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, Q} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(a+7 \\not\\equiv b+7 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, P}$,\nwhich is equivalent to\n$a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a+7 \\equiv b+7 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$.\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> The contrapositive of the statement is\n> $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a+7 \\equiv b+7 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$.\n> We will prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the definition of congruence modulo $n$. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$. So, we can reason as follows:\n\n> Since $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$, by the definition of modular congruence,\n> $10 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad a-b = 10k$\n> for some integer $k$.\n\nNow, we express the difference between $a+7$ and $b+7$ through the difference between $a$ and $b{:}$\n\n> Now, notice that\n> $(a + 7) - (b + 7)|= a + 7 - b - 7; = a - b; = 10k$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence in reverse:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have\n> $a+7 \\equiv b+7 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$.\n> By proving the contrapositive, we proved the initial statement, as required." #edb/ref 13194139533368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157065 "Recall that the conditional statement $P \\Rightarrow Q$ and its contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nare logically equivalent. Moreover, it's sometimes easier to prove the contrapositive.\n\nIn this case, we need to show that\n$\\underbrace{a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)}_{P} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{4a \\not\\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12)}_{Q}$.\nThe contrapositive of the statement is\n$\\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(4a \\not\\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, Q} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, P}$,\nwhich is equivalent to\n$4a \\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$.\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> The contrapositive of the given statement is\n> $4a \\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$.\n> We will prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the definition of modular congruence. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$. So, we can reason as follows:\n\n> By the definition of modular congruence,\n> $4a≡4b(mod12) \\mid ⇔12∣(4a - 4b); ⇔12∣4(a - b)$\n\nNext, we use the definition of divisibility:\n\n> By the definition of divisibility in $\\mathbb{Z}$, $12 \\mid 4(a-b)$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that\n> $4(a-b) = 12k$.\n\nNow, we can cancel out the factor of $4{:}$\n\n> Dividing this equation by $4$, we obtain\n> $a-b = 3k$,\n> which in turn implies\n> $3 \\mid (a-b)$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence again:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have\n> $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$.\n> By proving the contrapositive, we proved the initial statement, as required." #edb/ref 13194139533368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157127 "Let $X$ be the set of solutions of\n\n$$\nx + y +z =11\n$$\n\nin the non-negative integers. We can present this equation in the form\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots +1}_{x}+\\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots +1}_{y}+\\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots +1}_{z}=11\n$$\n\nThe problem of finding the number of solutions is analogous to finding the number of ways to distribute $11$ identical balls (the $1$'s) into $3$ boxes ($x$, $y$, and $z$). So, the number of solutions is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle | X | & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11 + 3 - 1}{11} \\right) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{13}{11} \\right) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{13 !}{11 ! 2 !}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 78.\\end{array}}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186157128 "First, we create an equivalent equation without a constraint. To do that, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=9+x'\n$$\n\nwhere $x'$ is a non-negative integer. Then the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y + z &= 20 \\\\\n(9 + x^{′}) + y + z &= 20 \\\\\nx^{′} + y + z &= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can carry on as usual. Let $X$ be the set of solutions of\n\n$$\nx' + y +z =11\n$$\n\nin the non-negative integers. We can present this equation in the form\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots +1}_{x'}+\\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots +1}_{y}+\\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots +1}_{z}=11\n$$\n\nThe problem of finding the number of solutions is analogous to finding the number of ways to distribute $11$ identical balls (the $1$'s) into $3$ boxes ($x'$, $y$, and $z$). So, the number of solutions is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle | X | & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11 + 3 - 1}{11} \\right) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{13}{11} \\right) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{13 !}{11 ! 2 !}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 78.\\end{array}}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186166487 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z = 2x+2y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = 0, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+2y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,0,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2y+0x+2x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2}{3}x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}x\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[y]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{x}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186166503 "Recall that if $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t),$ then $\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t),$ $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t),\\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\nc_1 \\mathbf{x}_{1}(t) + c_2\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t) + \\cdots + c_k\\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith $c_1, c_2, \\ldots, c_k \\in\\mathbb R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 13194139533389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171735 "Let's write down the formal statement that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{n^4-1\\textrm{is even}}_{P}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{n\\textrm{is odd}}_{Q}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is true, we assume $P$ and $\\lnot Q$ are both true and show that this leads to a contradiction.\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $n^4-1$ is even and $n$ is even. Then $n = 2a$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression $n^4-1$ and obtain a contradiction.\n\n> As a result,\n> $n^{4} - 1|= (2a)^{4} - 1; = 16a^{4} - 1; = (16a^{4} - 2) + 1; = 2(8a^{4} - 1)_(⏟)_(b) + 1$.\n\nIf we define another integer $b=8a^4-1$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nn^4-1 = 2b+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $n^4-1$ is one more than a multiple of $2$. But since $n^4-1$ is even, we've reached a contradiction.\n\n> Let $b=8a^4-1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> So, we have\n> $n^{4} - 1|= 2(8a^{4} - 1)_(⏟)_(b) + 1; = 2b + 1$,\n> which is one larger than a multiple of $2$. But this is a contradiction since $n^4-1$ is even.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our assumption that $\\lnot Q$ is true is incorrect. Hence, $Q$ is true, and we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $n$ is odd." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171738 "According to the definition, \n![](images/90/90f05ddf350a2b7a739a219c2dbb35958fd182ee9e665e0e5f6ce317ba7675ed.svg)\n\nSince $7 \\times 7 = 49,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7 \\times 7}_{\\color{blue}49} \\times \\underbrace{7 \\times 7}_{\\color{blue}49} = {\\color{blue}49} \\times {\\color{blue}49}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $49 \\times 49.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\color{blue}3[/math]}} \\\\ \\boxed{\\text{[math]\\color{blue}8[/math]}}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7^4=2,401.$" #edb/ref 13194139534642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171751 "Let's write down the formal statement that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3n+1\\textrm{is even}}_{P}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{n\\textrm{is odd}}_{Q}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is true, we assume $P$ and $\\lnot Q$ are both true and show that this leads to a contradiction.\n\nWe start by assuming that $3n+1$ is even and $n$ is even.\n\n> Assume for a contradiction that $3n+1$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{even}}$ and $n$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{even}}$.\n> Then, $n=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2a}$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression $3n+1$ and show that this implies $3n+1$ is odd.\n\n> Substituting $n=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2a}$ into the expression $3n+1$ implies that $3n+1$ is odd.\n\nThis gives our contradiction.\n\n> But this is a $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{contradiction}}$ since $3n+1$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{even}}$ by assumption.\n\nTherefore, the assumption that $\\lnot Q$ is true was incorrect, which means that $Q$ is true.\n\n> Therefore, we conclude that if $3n+1$ is even, then $n$ is is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171834 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\: \\underbrace{ 2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2}_{Q(\\mathbf{x})} -2x_1+2x_2 =7\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n![](images/78/783b32a7595534982e5fb057c049081564807b9d47988670c7d30058fb3900a0.png)\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(\\mathbf x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2})+2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable $y_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}) & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2})+2 & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2})+(\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+2 & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =5 \\\\ 5{z}_{1}^{2}-{z}_{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt5.$" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186179451 "Let's start by multiplying the first two numbers. We have the product of two negatives, which gives a positive: \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{red}(-5)} = {\\color{blue}15}.\n$$\n\nSo we now have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{red}(-5)}}_{\\large {\\color{blue}15}} \\cdot {\\color{red}(-10)} = {\\color{blue}15} \\cdot {\\color{red}(-10)}.\n$$\n\nThen, we work out this remaining product using the fact that a positive times a negative gives a negative:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} \\cdot {\\color{red}(-10)} = {\\color{red}-(15\\cdot 10)} = {\\color{red}-150}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186179491 "The initial fuel volume in the tank is $8{,}000$ liters.\n\n- After $1$ minute, there will be $8{,}000 - 125$ liters in the tank.\n- After $\\color{blue}2$ minutes, there will be $8{,}000 \\underbrace{\\:-\\: 125 - 125}_{{-\\,125\\,\\cdot\\, \\color{blue}{2}}}$ liters in the tank.\n- After $\\color{blue}3$ minutes, there will be $8{,}000 \\underbrace{\\:-\\: 125 - 125 - 125}_{{-\\,125\\,\\cdot\\, \\color{blue}{3}}}$ liters in the tank.$\\qquad\\vdots$\n- After $\\color{blue}t$ minutes, there will be $8{,}000 \\underbrace{\\:-\\: 125 - \\cdots - 125}_{{-\\,125\\,\\cdot\\, \\color{blue}{t}}}$ liters in the tank.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nF(t)=8{,}000 - 125t\n$$\n\nwhich is a linear function.\n\nFinally, since the slope $(-125)$ is negative, $F$ decreases as $t$ increases.\nTherefore, $F$ is a decreasing linear function." #edb/ref 13194139533373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186179501 "**Method 1**\n\nSince we have an even number of data points ($6$), the median of the data set is the mean of the two middle numbers (i.e., the $\\color{blue}3$rd and $\\color{blue}4$th numbers). The upper part of the data contains the $4$th number and all numbers above it.\n\n![](images/6e/6e0110307df3ae02088c1bccc3c9f28ddedf1c9ebf22262360f0f2e2dafc7710.png)\n\nNow, we find the median of the upper part. In this case, it's just the middle number:\n\n![](images/83/838fad9e4d35ebd000c22b384744223a627ca3e6bc894abfd24d36abba8e5f26.png)\n\nSo, the upper quartile is ${\\color{red}{7}}$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nThe dot plot tells us the following:\n\n- There are $\\color{blue}2$ values equal to $5$.\n- There are $\\color{blue}2$ values equal to $6$.\n- There are $\\color{blue}2$ values equal to $7$.\n\nSo, we get the following data:\n\n$$\n5, \\: 5, \\: 6, \\: 6, \\: 7, \\: 7\n$$\n\nSince we have an even number of data points ($6$), the median of the data set is the mean of the two middle numbers (i.e., the $\\color{blue}3$rd and $\\color{blue}4$th numbers). The upper part of the data contains the 4th number and all numbers above it.\n\n$$\n5, \\: 5, \\: 6, \\: \\mid \\: \\underbrace{6, \\: 7, \\: 7}_{\\text{upper part}}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the median of the upper half. In this case, it's just the middle number:\n$6, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{7}}, \\: 7$\n\nSo, the upper quartile is ${\\color{red}{7}}$." #edb/ref 13194139533373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186179502 "**Method 1**\n\nNotice that the number of data points ($7$) is odd. As a result, we can separate the data into its lower and upper parts, as illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5aeae6a24bfc2b294e9cdbc6a8829cfeb5935f267f70e60e03351bd78c28a28.png)\n\n- The lower quartile is the median of the lower part. In this case, it's just the middle number. Therefore,\n$\\text{lower quartile} = 8$,\nas shown in the diagram on the left.\n- The upper quartile is the median of the upper part. In this case, it's also the middle number. Therefore,\n$\\text{upper quartile} = 16$,\nas shown in the diagram on the right.\n\nSo, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\text{upper quartile} - \\text{lower quartile} = 16 - 8 = 8\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nThe dot plot represents the following data:\n\n$$\n4, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\: 16, \\: 16, \\: 16, \\: 20\n$$\n\nSince the number of data points is odd ($7$), the median of the data set is the middle number (the $\\color{blue}4$th value).\n\nNow, we find the quartiles.\n\n- The lower part consists of every data point below the median:\n$\\underbrace{4, \\: 8, \\: 12,}_{\\text{lower part}} \\: {\\color{blue}16}, \\: 16, \\: 16, \\: 20$\n- The lower quartile is the median of the lower half. In this case, it's just the middle number:\n$4, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{8}}, \\: 12$\nTherefore,\n$\\text{lower quartile} = 8$.\n- The upper part consists of every data point above the median:\n$8, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\:{\\color{blue}16}, \\: \\underbrace{16, \\: 16, \\: 20}_{\\text{upper part}}$\nThe upper quartile is the median of the upper part. In this case, it's also just the middle number:\n$16, \\:{\\color{red}\\underline{16}}, \\: 20$\nTherefore,\n$\\text{upper quartile} = 16$.\n\nFinally, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\text{upper quartile} - \\text{lower quartile} = 16 - 8 = 8\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186179515 "First, we notice that \n\n$$\nk! = k \\cdot (k-1)!\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify the numerator as follows:\n\n$$\nk^2 (k-1)! = k \\cdot \\underbrace{k \\cdot (k-1)!}_{k!} = k \\cdot k!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $k!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {k^2(k - 1)!} {k!} \n&= \\dfrac {k \\cdot k!} {k!}\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {k \\cdot \\cancel{k!}} {\\cancel{k!}} \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {k} {1} \\[5pt]\n&= k\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186179620 "**Method 1**\n\nSince we have an odd number of data points ($11$), the median of the data set is the middle number (i.e., the $\\color{blue}6$th number). The upper part of the data contains everything above the median.\n\n![](images/53/53379813bd3a8bb3e6f5f8995ca27ebce314cc73ff0885f0e5ceba372e09120c.png)\n\nTo compute the upper quartile, we find the median of the upper part. In this case, it's the middle number:\n\n![](images/e8/e84937a8da944cb18f3ddf6262e0ef1f743a4f039cb1bb19268b826ed5460665.png)\n\nSo, the upper quartile is ${\\color{red}{45}}$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nThe dot plot tells us the following:\n\n- There are $\\color{blue}2$ items corresponding to the number $25$.\n- There are $\\color{blue}3$ items corresponding to the number $30$.\n- There are $\\color{blue}2$ items corresponding to the number $35$.\n- There is $\\color{blue}1$ item corresponding to the number $40$.\n- There are $\\color{blue}3$ items corresponding to the number $45$.\n\nSo, we get the following data:\n\n$$\n25, \\: 25, \\: 30, \\: 30, \\: 30, \\: 35, \\: 35, \\: 40, \\: 45, \\: 45, \\: 45\n$$\n\nSince we have an odd number of data points ($11$), the median of the data set is the middle number (i.e., the $\\color{blue}6$th number). The upper part of the data contains everything above the median.\n\n$$\n25, \\: 25, \\: 30, \\: 30, \\: 30, \\: {\\color{blue}35}, \\: \\underbrace{35, \\: 40, \\: 45, \\: 45, \\: 45}_{\\text{upper part}}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the median of the upper part. In this case, it's the middle number:\n$35, \\: 40, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{45}}, \\: 45, \\: 45$\n\nSo, the upper quartile is ${\\color{red}{45}}$." #edb/ref 13194139533373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186183943 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(x^2 -7x +13)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 -7x+13\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x +{\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{13}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2} -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-  {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2  -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-  {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2  +\\dfrac{3}4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+7x-13 & =-({(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186183944 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2\\left(x^2 - 3x + \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 3x + {\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2} }\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x +  {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2} }$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-3x+\\frac{1}{2}) & =2({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{4} \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186183962 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{-1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\dfrac{53}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186183963 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\dfrac{49}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186184028 "We start by connecting the task of finding the number of functions to finding the number of possible tables (one table for each possible function).\n\n> Each combination of our Boolean variables can be uniquely represented by a tuple of length $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nWe first need to determine the number of rows in a table to find the number of possible tables.\n\n> Now, each coordinate of the tuple can have one of $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ possible values. Thus, by the combinatorial rule of $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{product}}$, there are $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^{3}}$ possible tuples in total.\n\nNotice that the number of tuples was computed as\n\n$$\n{2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2}= 2^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the number of possible tables.\n\n> As a result, our table has $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^{3}}$ rows. Here, each row contains one of $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ possible function values. Therefore, by the combinatorial rule of $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{product}}$, there are $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^{2^3}}$ possible functions in total.\n\nNotice that the number of tuples was computed as $\\underbrace{2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 2}_{ { { {2}^{3}} \\textrm{ times}}} = 2^{2^3}.$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186184115 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(x^2 - 6x -1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 6x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2- {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 10\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x+1 & =-((x-3)^{2}-10) \\\\  & =-(x-3)^{2}+10 \\\\  & =10-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186184116 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2\\left(x^2 - 10x + 20\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 10x  + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}2}$ as a factor: \n\n$$\nx^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue} 5}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}20}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{blue} 5}^2} - {\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 -{\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 - 5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}-10x+20) & =-2({(x-5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186184448 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}-16k+8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}-2\\cdot 8k+8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${8}^{2}$, writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{k}^{2}-2\\cdot 8k+{8}^{2}}-{8}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(k-8)^{2}-{8}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(k-8)^{2}-56.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({k}^{2}-16k+8) & =-({(k-8)}^{2}-56) \\\\  & =-(k-8)^{2}+56 \\\\  & =56-(k-8)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186184483 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-5y+6\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+6\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $6$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+6\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-5y+6) & =-({(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186184484 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-9$. So, we take out a factor of $-9$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-9({z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z-1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9({z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1) & =-9({(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+(-9)\\cdot (-\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186184485 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}+x+3)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+3\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $3$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-x-3 & =-({(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186184804 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $8$. So, we take out a factor of $8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n8({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x) & =8({(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186184805 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $3$. So, we take out a factor of $3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n3({y}^{2}+2y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 1y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$, writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}}-{1}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({y}^{2}+2y+5) & =3({(y+1)}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =3{(y+1)}^{2}+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3{(y+1)}^{2}+12\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186184806 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3$. So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{3}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}) & =-3({(y-1)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186188002 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-y/2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-y/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y \\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-2{e}^{-y/2}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-2{e}^{-b/2}+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right].$ So, when $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right]$ and $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right),$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}}_{\\large f(x,y)} = \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_X(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_Y(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 13194139535179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186188015 "Since the missing number lies to the *left* of the number $5,285$, it must be *less than* this number. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0000}}} <       5,285.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our numbers in turn.\n\n- The number $485$ satisfies the condition since it has fewer digits than $5,285$.\n$\\underbrace{485}_{\\color{blue}{ {3} \\textrm{ digits}}} <       \\underbrace{5,285}_{\\color{blue}{ {4} \\textrm{ digits}}}$\n- The number $6,567$ does *not* satisfies the condition. Let's compare place values, *going from left to right*:In the *hundreds* place, we see that $6 > 5$. **thousands****hundreds****tens****ones**$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{5}$$2$$8$$5$$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{6}$$5$$6$$7$So, $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{6},567 >\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{5},285$.\n- The number $10,001$ does *not* satisfy the condition since it has more digits than $5,285$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186192387 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 1.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.90=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.25 \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{8} + \\dfrac{2.4^2}{42} \\bigg)} \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  1.6 \\pm 1.1\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186192410 "We're given that\n\n$$\ng(x) = x\\cdot f(x) > 0\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have $f'(x) \\gt 0$.\n- Statement II is false. Differentiating $g(x) = x \\cdot f(x)$, we get\n$g^{′}(x)|= (x)^{′} \\cdot f(x) + x \\cdot f^{′}(x); = f(x) + x \\cdot f^{′}(x)$.\nNow, since $f(x)$ is negative and strictly increasing over the interval $(-2,-1)$, we have\n$f(x) \\lt 0, \\qquad x \\lt 0, \\qquad f'(x) \\gt 0$.\nHence, we obtain\n$g'(x) = \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\lt 0} + \\underbrace{x}_{< 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} = \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\lt 0} + \\underbrace{x \\cdot f'(x)}_{\\lt 0} \\lt 0$.\n- Statement III is false. Since $g'(x) \\lt 0$, the function $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing. Therefore, the left Riemann sum gives an *overerestimate* of the area under the curve $y = x \\cdot f(x)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 13194139533376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186192421 "Using the constant multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{100}\\dfrac14 =\\dfrac14\\cdot \\sum_{i=1}^{100} 1\n$$\n\nThen, we note that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{100} 1 = \\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots + 1}_{100\\,\\textrm{times}} = 100\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14\\cdot \\sum_{i=1}^{100} 1 = \\dfrac14\\cdot 100= 25\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186192490 "First, let's simplify our diagram, keeping only the relevant information:\n\n![](images/29/29502b2df474fd3da9b08d5760a3a80468dc2d366b88b7b3be5a22ce82e8d4f4.png)\n\nNow, let's recall the SAS similarity criterion:\n\n> *Two triangles are similar if the ratio of the lengths of two pairs of corresponding sides is the same and the angles included by these sides are congruent.*\n\nNotice that $\\triangle BOA$ and $\\triangle MON$ have a pair of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\angle BOA \\cong \\angle MON\n$$\n\nLet's list the sides that enclose the congruent angle for each triangle, from smallest to largest:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{({\\color{blue}{9}},{\\color{blue}{12}})}_{\\triangle BOA}, \\qquad \\underbrace{({\\color{red}{12}},{\\color{red}{16}})}_{\\triangle MON}\n$$\n\nNotice that the ratios of these lengths are in proportion:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{\\color{red}12} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}12}{\\color{red}16} = \\dfrac34\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\triangle BOA \\sim \\triangle MON$ by the SAS criterion." #edb/ref 13194139533376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186192562 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\ncan be viewed as the sum of $9$ instances of the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} \n}^{\\large { {9} \\textrm{ times}}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nExamining the known fractions on the left-hand side of the given equation, from left to right, we have the following:\n\n- $2$ instances of $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ gives the fraction $\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{10}$\n- $4$ instances of $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ gives the fraction $\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{10}$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t\\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10}}_{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{10}} + \\underbrace{\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}}_{\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{10}} +\n\t\\color{violet}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}  \n}^{\\large { {9} \\textrm{ times}}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nWe have $3$ instances of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1{10}\n$$\n\nremaining, and these give the fraction\n\n$$\n{\\color{violet}{\\dfrac3{10}}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t\\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} }_{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{10}} + \n\t\\underbrace{\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10} }_{\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{10}} + \n\t\\underbrace{\\color{violet}\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+ \\dfrac{1}{10}}_{\\color{violet}\\dfrac{3}{10}}\n}^{\\large { {9} \\textrm{ times}}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nThis gives the decomposition\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{10}} + {\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{10}}+ \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\color{violet}\\dfrac{3}{10}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing fraction is\n\n$$\n{\\color{violet}{\\dfrac3{10}}}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186192564 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\ncan be viewed as the sum of the whole and fractional parts:\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} = 3 + \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nIn turn, the whole number $3$ can be written as\n\n$$\n3 = 1 + 1 + 1= \\dfrac{4}{4} + \\dfrac{4}{4} + \\dfrac{4}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{4}{4} + \\dfrac{4}{4} + \\dfrac{4}{4}}_{\\large 3} + \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{4+4+4+1}{4} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}13}{4}\n$$\n\nAs a result, any decomposition of\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nmust contain fractions\n\n- whose denominator is $4$, and\n- the sum of all numerators gives ${\\color{blue}13}$.\n\nAmong the given options, the only fraction with these properties is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nIndeed, its denominator is $4$ and\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\dfrac{5}{4}} + \\dfrac{2}{4} + \\dfrac{6}{4} = \\dfrac{5+2+6}{4} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}13}{4}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186198100 "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\land,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is positive and a multiple of $4.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x \\,\\textrm{is positive}}_P \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{x \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is a multiple of }4 }_M,\n$$\n\nthat is, $P \\land M.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is negative. This corresponds to $N.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x \\,\\textrm{ is positive}\\,\\textrm{and a multiple of }4 }_{P \\land M} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{x \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is negative} }_N,\n$$\n\nthat is, $(P \\land M) \\lor N.$" #edb/ref 13194139533457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186203674 "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b){:} \\quad 3a \\equiv 3b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$\n\n- $Q(a,b){:} \\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: P \\Rightarrow Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n3a \\equiv 3b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6) \n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\na \\not\\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6).\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b) =(0,2)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na = 0, \\qquad\nb = 2.\n$$\n\nThen, $3a=0$ and $3b=6.$ \n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{0}_{3a} \\equiv \\underbrace{6}_{3b} \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)\n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n\\underbrace{0}_{a} \\not\\equiv \\underbrace{2}_{b} \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=0$ and $b=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 13194139534331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186203854 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax = b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $4x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+7 & =-4x+5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =-4x+5+4x \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+4x+7 & =5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7-7 & =5-7 \\\\ 2nx+4x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2n + 4)x  = -2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2n + 4)}_{a}x  = \\underbrace{-2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2n + 4= 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+4 & =0 \\\\ 2n+4-4 & =0-4 \\\\ 2n & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{2n}{2} & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\ n & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186203878 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-9)z+2 & =-1 \\\\ (r-9)z+2-2 & =-1-2 \\\\ \\underbrace{(r-9)}_{a}z & =\\underbrace{-3}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nr - 9 = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r-9 & =0 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186204597 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-4)x+2 & =5 \\\\ 2(p-4)x+2-2 & =5-2 \\\\ \\underbrace{2(p-4)}_{a}x & =\\underbrace{3}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(p-4)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(p-4)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ p-4 & =0 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186204598 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $3x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2px & =3x+5 \\\\ 2px-3x & =3x+5-3x \\\\ 2px-3x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2p-3)x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2p-3)}_{a}x=\\underbrace{5}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2p-3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-3 & =0 \\\\ 2p-3+3 & =0+3 \\\\ 2p & =3 \\\\ \\frac{2p}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186208477 "When a new data point is added, the mean of the dataset will change, and the median of the dataset may change:\n\n- The mean of the dataset moves toward that new data point.\n- The median of the dataset moves toward that new data point or stays the same.\n\nIn dataset $M$, all values are in the range $20$ and $60$, so the mean and the median are in this range.\n\n- Dataset $N$ is formed by adding the value $500$ to $M$. Since $500$ is far above every value in $M$ (all are at most $60$), the new mean for $N$ must be *greater* than the mean of $M$.So, the mean must be greater for dataset $N$ than for dataset $M$, meaning statement I is true and statement III is false.\n- To find the median, we list the dataset $M$ in ascending order:\n$M_1 \\le \\dots \\le M_{52}$\nDataset $M$ has $52$ values, so the median is the average of the $26$th and $27$th values, $\\dfrac{M_{26}+M_{27}}{2}$. On the other hand, dataset $N$ has $53$ values:\n$\\underbrace{M_1}_{N_1} \\le \\dots \\le \\underbrace{M_{52}}_{N_{52}} < \\underbrace{500}_{N_{53}}$\nSo, the median value is the $27$th value:\n$\\text{median}(N)=N_{27}=M_{27}\\ \\ \\text{and}\\ \\ \\text{median}(M)=\\frac{M_{26}+M_{27}}{2}\\le M_{27}=\\text{median}(N)$.\nThe inequality can be strict (if $M_{26} < M_{27}$) or an equality (if $M_{26}=M_{27}$).As a result, the median might not be greater for dataset $N$ than for dataset $M$, meaning statement IV is true and statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **“I and IV only.”**" #edb/ref 13194139533379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186212760 "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(y - 1)^3(y + 2) + (y + 4)(y - 1)^2$.\n\nNotice that both terms in this expression contain a common factor of $(y - 1)^2$. So, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(y - 1)^2\\left[(y - 1)(y + 2) + (y + 4)\\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\phantom{=} ( y - 1 {)}^{2} [ ( y - 1 ) ( y + 2 ) + ( y + 4 ) ] \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( y - 1 {)}^{2} [ {y}^{2} + y - 2 + y + 4 ] \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( y - 1 {)}^{2} \\cdot  \\underset{g ( y )}{\\underbrace{( {y}^{2} + 2 y + 2 )}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ng(y) = y^2 + 2y + 2\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186216958 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The current density of a wire is defined as the **amount of electric current,** in microamperes, that passes perpendicularly through the wire’s cross-sectional surface, **divided by** the **area of that surface**, measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $J$ is the current density, in microamperes per square meter.\n- $I$ is the current, in microamperes\n- $A$ is the cross-sectional area, in square meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that the current density is the current divided by the area:\n\n$$\nJ = \\frac{I}{A}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of this formula:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{microamperes per square meter}}_{\\textrm{units of} J} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{microamperes}}{\\textrm{square meters}}}_{\\textrm{units of} I/A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $I = 12$ microamperes\n- $A = 0.004$ square meters\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ &= \\frac{12microamperes}{0.004square meters} \\\\\n&= 3000microamperes per square meter\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the current density is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3000}$ microamperes per square meter." #edb/ref 13194139533381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186216963 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The magnetic flux density on a surface is defined as the **magnetic flux**, in webers, passing perpendicularly through the surface, **divided by** the **area of the surface**, measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $B$ is the magnetic flux density, in webers per square meter.\n- $\\Phi$ is the magnetic flux, in webers.\n- $A$ is the area of the surface, in square meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that\n\n$$\nB = \\frac{\\Phi}{A}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of this formula:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{webers per square meter}}_{\\textrm{units of} B} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{webers}}{\\textrm{square meters}}}_{\\textrm{units of} \\Phi/A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $B = 10$ webers per square meter\n- $A = 7$ square meters\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB &= \\frac{Φ}{A} \\\\\n10 &= \\frac{Φ}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$, we find\n\n$$\n\\Phi = 10 \\cdot 7 = 70\\,\\,\\textrm{webers}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnetic flux is $70$ webers." #edb/ref 13194139533381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186216966 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The magnetic flux of a magnetic field passing perpendicularly through a surface is defined as the **product** of the **magnetic field strength**, in webers per square meter, and the **area of the surface,** measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $\\Phi$ is the magnetic flux, in webers.\n- $B$ is the magnetic field strength, in webers per square meter.\n- $A$ is the area of the surface, in square meters.\n- $\\ell$ is the length of the rectangle, in meters.\n- $w$ is the width of the rectangle, in meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that\n\n$$\n\\Phi = B \\cdot A\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{webers}}_{\\textrm{units of} \\Phi} = \\underbrace{(\\textrm{webers per square meter})(\\textrm{square meters})}_{\\textrm{units of} B \\cdot A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nSince the width is four times the length, we have $w = 4\\ell$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\ell \\cdot w = \\ell \\cdot (4\\ell) = 4\\ell^2\n$$\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $\\ell = 1.5$ meters\n- $B = 4$ weber per square meter\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = 4 \\cdot (1.5)^2 = 9\\ \\text{square meters}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula for $\\Phi$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ &= 4 \\cdot 9 = 36webers\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnetic flux is $36$ webers." #edb/ref 13194139533381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186216970 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The force exerted on a stretched spring is defined as the **product** of the **spring constant**, in newtons per meter, and the **extension of the spring**, in meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $F$ is the force exerted on the spring, in newtons.\n- $k$ is the spring constant, in newtons per meter.\n- $\\Delta x$ is the extension of the spring, in meters.\n- $L_0$ is the natural length of the spring, in meters.\n- $L$ is the stretched length of the spring, in meters.\n\nThe extension of the spring equals the change in length of the spring:\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = L - L_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force is related to the extension by\n\n$$\nF = k \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{newtons}}_{\\textrm{units of} F} = \\underbrace{(\\textrm{newtons per meter})(\\textrm{meters})}_{\\textrm{units of} k \\cdot \\Delta x}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following information:\n\n- $F = 30$ newtons\n- $k = 120$ newtons per meter\n- $L_0 = 0.46$ meters\n\nRearranging the formula for the extension, we get\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\frac{F}{k}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔx &= \\frac{30}{120} = 0.25m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the stretched length of the spring is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= L_{0} + Δx \\\\\n&= 0.46 + 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0.71m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stretched length of the spring is $0.71$ meters." #edb/ref 13194139533381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186216971 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The specific heat capacity of a substance is defined as the amount of energy, in joules $(\\textrm{J})$, required to raise the temperature of $1$ gram $(\\textrm{g})$ of the substance by $1$ degree Celsius $(^\\circ\\textrm{C})$.\n\nWe're also given the following information:\n\n> The specific heat capacity of copper is approximately $0.39 \\,\\tfrac{\\textrm{J}}{\\textrm{g} \\cdot {}^\\circ \\textrm{C}}$.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $Q$ is the energy required, in joules $(\\textrm{J})$\n- $m$ is the mass of the substance, in grams $(\\textrm{g})$\n- $\\Delta T$ is the **change** in temperature, in degrees Celsius $({}^\\circ\\textrm{C})$\n- $c$ is the specific heat capacity, in joules per gram per degree Celsius $\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{J}}{\\textrm{g} \\cdot {}^\\circ \\textrm{C}}\\right)$\n\nNotice that the units of $c$ are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{J}}{\\textrm{g} \\cdot {}^\\circ \\textrm{C}}\n$$\n\nwhich suggests that our variables are related by the following formula:\n\n$$\nc = \\dfrac{Q}{m\\Delta T}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of this formula:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{J}}{\\textrm{g} \\cdot {}^\\circ \\textrm{C}}}_{\\textrm{units of} c} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{J}}{\\textrm{g} \\cdot {}^\\circ \\textrm{C}}}_{\\textrm{units of} Q/m\\Delta T}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following information:\n\n- $m = 100\\,\\textrm{g}$\n- $c = 0.39 \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{J}}{\\textrm{g} \\cdot {}^\\circ \\textrm{C}}$\n- Initial temperature $= 15\\,^\\circ\\!\\textrm{C}$\n- Final temperature $= 40\\,^\\circ\\!\\textrm{C}$\n\nSo, the temperature change is\n\n$$\n\\Delta T = 40 - 15 = 25\\,^\\circ\\!\\textrm{C}\n$$\n\nSince we wish to find the energy required, $Q$, let's make $Q$ the subject in our formula. This gives\n\n$$\nQ = mc\\Delta T\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ &= 100 \\cdot 0.39 \\cdot 25 \\\\\n&= 975J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the energy required is approximately $975$ joules." #edb/ref 13194139533381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186221092 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = b$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n$$\nb\\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf $a = 0$, then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax = b$.\n\nWe start by adding $4x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2nx + 7 &= -4x + 5 \\\\\n2nx + 4x + 7 &= -4x + 5 + 4x \\\\\n2nx + 4x + 7 &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2nx + 4x + 7 &= 5 \\\\\n2nx + 4x + 7 - 7 &= 5 - 7 \\\\\n2nx + 4x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2n + 4)x = -2\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2n + 4)}_{a}x = \\underbrace{-2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2n + 4= 0\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2n + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2n + 4 - 4 &= 0 - 4 \\\\\n2n &= -4 \\\\\n\\frac{2n}{2} &= \\frac{-4}{2} \\\\\nn &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186224892 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{z - 30}{6}\n$$\n\nand asked to rewrite it in the form\n\n$$\nz = p u + q\n$$\n\nin order to determine the value of $p$.\n\nFirst, we multiply *both sides* of the initial equation by $6$ to eliminate the fraction:\n$6u = z - 30$\n\nThen, we add *$30$ to both sides* to solve for $z{:}$\n\n$$\nz = 6u + 30\n$$\n\nFinally, we identify the coefficient of $u$. The equation is now in the form\n\n$$\nz = \\underbrace{6}_{p} \\cdot u + \\underbrace{30}_{q}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $p$ is $6$." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186225049 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The current density of a wire is defined as the **amount of electric current,** in microamperes, that passes perpendicularly through the wire’s cross-sectional surface, **divided by** the **area of that surface**, measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $J$ is the current density, in microamperes per square meter.\n- $I$ is the current, in microamperes\n- $A$ is the cross-sectional area, in square meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that the current density is the current divided by the area:\n\n$$\nJ = \\frac{I}{A}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of this formula:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{microamperes per square meter}}_{\\textrm{units of} J} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{microamperes}}{\\textrm{square meters}}}_{\\textrm{units of} I/A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $I = 12$ microamperes\n- $A = 0.004$ square meters\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ &= \\frac{12microamperes}{0.004square meters} \\\\\n&= 3000microamperes per square meter\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the current density is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3000}$ microamperes per square meter." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186225050 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The magnetic flux density on a surface is defined as the **magnetic flux**, in webers, passing perpendicularly through the surface, **divided by** the **area of the surface**, measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $B$ is the magnetic flux density, in webers per square meter.\n- $\\Phi$ is the magnetic flux, in webers.\n- $A$ is the area of the surface, in square meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that\n\n$$\nB = \\frac{\\Phi}{A}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of this formula:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{webers per square meter}}_{\\textrm{units of} B} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{webers}}{\\textrm{square meters}}}_{\\textrm{units of} \\Phi/A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $B = 10$ webers per square meter\n- $A = 7$ square meters\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB &= \\frac{Φ}{A} \\\\\n10 &= \\frac{Φ}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$, we find\n\n$$\n\\Phi = 10 \\cdot 7 = 70\\,\\,\\textrm{webers}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnetic flux is $70$ webers." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186225051 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The force exerted on a stretched spring is defined as the **product** of the **spring constant**, in newtons per meter, and the **extension of the spring**, in meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $F$ is the force exerted on the spring, in newtons.\n- $k$ is the spring constant, in newtons per meter.\n- $\\Delta x$ is the extension of the spring, in meters.\n- $L_0$ is the natural length of the spring, in meters.\n- $L$ is the stretched length of the spring, in meters.\n\nThe extension of the spring equals the change in length of the spring:\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = L - L_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force is related to the extension by\n\n$$\nF = k \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{newtons}}_{\\textrm{units of} F} = \\underbrace{(\\textrm{newtons per meter})(\\textrm{meters})}_{\\textrm{units of} k \\cdot \\Delta x}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following information:\n\n- $F = 30$ newtons\n- $k = 120$ newtons per meter\n- $L_0 = 0.46$ meters\n\nRearranging the formula for the extension, we get\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\frac{F}{k}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔx &= \\frac{30}{120} = 0.25m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the stretched length of the spring is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= L_{0} + Δx \\\\\n&= 0.46 + 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0.71m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stretched length of the spring is $0.71$ meters." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186225052 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The magnetic flux of a magnetic field passing perpendicularly through a surface is defined as the **product** of the **magnetic field strength**, in webers per square meter, and the **area of the surface,** measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $\\Phi$ is the magnetic flux, in webers.\n- $B$ is the magnetic field strength, in webers per square meter.\n- $A$ is the area of the surface, in square meters.\n- $\\ell$ is the length of the rectangle, in meters.\n- $w$ is the width of the rectangle, in meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that\n\n$$\n\\Phi = B \\cdot A\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{webers}}_{\\textrm{units of} \\Phi} = \\underbrace{(\\textrm{webers per square meter})(\\textrm{square meters})}_{\\textrm{units of} B \\cdot A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nSince the width is four times the length, we have $w = 4\\ell$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\ell \\cdot w = \\ell \\cdot (4\\ell) = 4\\ell^2\n$$\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $\\ell = 1.5$ meters\n- $B = 4$ weber per square meter\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = 4 \\cdot (1.5)^2 = 9\\ \\text{square meters}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula for $\\Phi$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ &= 4 \\cdot 9 = 36webers\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnetic flux is $36$ webers." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186228914 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/80/8004eea851883dbf0360ca3e5f39678967cdf5060668508f65cf3e5d54d6b027.svg)\nwhere ![](images/eb/ebe84b2cfbe7f95afe2c819b1d1d7790061c81744295076ac5bc739c13832dbe.svg) and ![](images/ed/edd1f7c4e50501b6849b37c98e822621d64d2f5d90a5de35b96d569814bb2a3a.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/5c/5c91edafde3ff3c119a58783296b9f8c56ecd3604d6b663af6d764a911fe1d72.svg)\n\nGiven the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x(t_0)$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n![](images/ed/ed77716162d9ecdeee50cd26172985ed4a5038cad903ca4729da1d9895fc9cc4.svg)\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t = t_0 = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x(0) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n![](images/22/22f92a117def6f3377a72f871d58d23cf339290a55c0f7d297ccb797b5046bf7.svg)\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3s+3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3s+3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3s+3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n![](images/34/348882682fbf17c25c759980898770a40422158cc3592d1fa51aa73a941f5cb6.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139535474 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186228916 "Given the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x(t_0)$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ![](images/7b/7b64128fdaf3565ee121779a8fcd2c84db8f639ded96f0884d6ad7e728c83b1c.svg) So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{4(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-1)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3(t-1){e}^{4(t-1)} \\\\ 3{e}^{4(t-1)}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n![](images/7f/7f1779e39ecb5e2bcce580d20a85eae97be505e2ad0987fa45b5b9e22c52fbbf.svg)\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4s} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3(t-1){e}^{4(t-1)} \\\\ 3{e}^{4(t-1)}\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535474 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186231329 "We rearrange the terms so that the like terms, $14m$ and $-5m$, are together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 14m - 6 - 5m \\\\[5pt] &= \\underbrace{14m - 5m}_{m \\textrm{terms}} - 6 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\underbrace{14m - 5m} - 6 \\\\[5pt] &= (14 - 5)m - 6 \\\\[5pt] &= 9m - 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186231330 "We have two groups of two like terms, $4m$ and $3m$, and $6n$ and $-2n$.\n\nTo collect these like terms, we combine each group into a single term, adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\underbrace{4m + 3m}_{m \\textrm{terms}}\\,+\\, \\underbrace{6n - 2n}_{n \\textrm{terms}} - 5 \\\\[5pt] &= (4+3)m + (6 - 2)n - 5 \\\\[5pt] &= 7m + 4n - 5 \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186233459 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+28\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $28$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}-\\,{6}^{2}+28\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+28\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-8.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272049 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{5}{4}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-11.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272050 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+8\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272052 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x-12\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x-12\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2}$, writing the constant term $12$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}-{2}^{2}-12\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-12\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-16.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+4x-12) & =-({(x+2)}^{2}-16) \\\\  & =-{(x+2)}^{2}+16 \\\\  & =16-(x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272054 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+10y+20\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 5y+20\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2}$, writing the constant term $20$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}-{5}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+5)^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}+5y+20) & =-({(y+5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-{(y+5)}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =5-(y+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272058 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+7x+10) & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272059 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-x-2) & =-({(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272060 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+9y+20\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+20\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $20$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}+9y+20) & =-({(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272061 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x+3\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $3$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+3x+3) & =-({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272062 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+5x+7\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+7\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $7$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+5x+7) & =-({(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272070 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6$. So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot x-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{11}{36}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36}) & =6({(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272081 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-5y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-5y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+5y-5 & =-({(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272124 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2$. So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-6x+8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2}$, writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}-{3}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-6x+8) & =2((x-3)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272127 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5$. So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+8x+12)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+12\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+12\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2}$, writing the constant term $12$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}}-{4}^{2}+12\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+12\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-4\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+8x+12) & =-5((x+4)^{2}-4) \\\\  & =-5{(x+4)}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272128 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5$. So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}) & =-5({(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272293 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $-2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272294 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+25\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $25$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nThe fi