b/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272298 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272299 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 7y+45\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${7}^{2}$, writing the constant term $45$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 7y+{7}^{2}}-\\,{7}^{2}+45\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+7)^{2}-{7}^{2}+45\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+7)^{2}-4.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272300 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+25\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $25$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272303 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+19\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2}$, writing the constant term $19$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}-\\,{5}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}-6.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272304 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+10\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2}$, writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}}-\\,{2}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-2)^{2}+6.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186272329 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-11y+30\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+30\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-11y+30) & =-({(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186290355 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+12x+14\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+14\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}-\\,{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-22.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+12x+14) & =-({(x+6)}^{2}-22) \\\\  & =-(x+6)^{2}+22 \\\\  & =22-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186290356 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}-16x-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-16x-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 8x-1\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${8}^{2}$, writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 8x+{8}^{2}}-\\,{8}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-8)^{2}-{8}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-8)^{2}-65.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+16x+1 & =-((x-8)^{2}-65) \\\\  & =-(x-8)^{2}+65 \\\\  & =65-(x-8)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186290358 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6$. So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 1p+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{6}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 1p+{1}^{2}}-\\,{1}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(p-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(p-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6}) & =6({(p-1)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =6{(p-1)}^{2}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =6{(p-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186290359 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8$. So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{29}{8}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}-\\,{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}) & =-8({(x-2)}^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3-8{(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186290408 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}-\\,{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186290409 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{w}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}w+3\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $3$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{w}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}w+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-\\,{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(w-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(w-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186290593 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 11y+131\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${11}^{2}$, writing the constant term $131$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 11y+{11}^{2}}-\\,{11}^{2}+131\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+11)^{2}-{11}^{2}+131\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+11)^{2}+10.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186290594 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 4y+13\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2}$, writing the constant term $13$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}-\\,{4}^{2}+13\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+13\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+4)^{2}-3.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186290595 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 12y+44\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${12}^{2}$, writing the constant term $44$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 12y+{12}^{2}}-\\,{12}^{2}+44\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-12)^{2}-{12}^{2}+44\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-12)^{2}-100.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186290596 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+53\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2}$, writing the constant term $53$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}-\\,{5}^{2}+53\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+53\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5)^{2}+28.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186290597 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+0\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2}$, writing the constant term $0$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}-\\,{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}-25\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=5$ and $q=-25$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186292540 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+5\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2}$, writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}-\\,{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2$ and $q=1$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186297201 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(p+2)x+7 & =10 \\\\ 3(p+2)x+7-7 & =10-7 \\\\ \\underbrace{3(p+2)}_{a}x & =\\underbrace{3}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3(p+2)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(p+2) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3(p+2)}{3} & =\\frac{0}{3} \\\\ p+2 & =0 \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186297202 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k-2)v-3 & =5 \\\\ (k-2)v-3+3 & =5+3 \\\\ \\underbrace{(k-2)}_{a}v & =\\underbrace{8}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nk-2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-2 & =0 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186297203 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(m+6)x+2 & =7 \\\\ (m+6)x+2-2 & =7-2 \\\\ \\underbrace{(m+6)}_{a}x & =\\underbrace{5}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nm+6=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m+6 & =0 \\\\ m & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186297204 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(q-5)u+9 & =0 \\\\ (q-5)u+9-9 & =0-9 \\\\ \\underbrace{(q-5)}_{a}u & =\\underbrace{-9}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q-5 & =0 \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186297205 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ay=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $5y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3py & =5y+8 \\\\ 3py-5y & =5y+8-5y \\\\ 3py-5y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $y$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3p-5)y=8.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ay=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(3p-5)}_{a}y=\\underbrace{8}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3p-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-5 & =0 \\\\ 3p-5+5 & =0+5 \\\\ 3p & =5 \\\\ \\frac{3p}{3} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186297206 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $az=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $7z$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3qz+4 & =7z+1 \\\\ 3qz-7z+4 & =7z+1-7z \\\\ 3qz-7z+4 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3qz-7z+4 & =1 \\\\ 3qz-7z+4-4 & =1-4 \\\\ 3qz-7z & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $z$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3q-7)z=-3.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(3q-7)}_{a}z=\\underbrace{-3}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3q-7=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3q-7 & =0 \\\\ 3q-7+7 & =0+7 \\\\ 3q & =7 \\\\ \\frac{3q}{3} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ q & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186297207 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $au=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $2u$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5ru+3 & =2u+6 \\\\ 5ru-2u+3 & =2u+6-2u \\\\ 5ru-2u+3 & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $3$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5ru-2u+3 & =6 \\\\ 5ru-2u+3-3 & =6-3 \\\\ 5ru-2u & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $u$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(5r-2)u=3.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(5r-2)}_{a}u=\\underbrace{3}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n5r-2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5r-2 & =0 \\\\ 5r-2+2 & =0+2 \\\\ 5r & =2 \\\\ \\frac{5r}{5} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186297208 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $aw=b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $3w$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4mw+5 & =-3w+1 \\\\ -4mw+3w+5 & =-3w+1+3w \\\\ -4mw+3w+5 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $5$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4mw+3w+5 & =1 \\\\ -4mw+3w+5-5 & =1-5 \\\\ -4mw+3w & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $w$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(-4m+3)w=-4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(-4m+3)}_{a}w=\\underbrace{-4}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-4m+3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4m+3 & =0 \\\\ -4m+3-3 & =0-3 \\\\ -4m & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{-4m}{-4} & =\\frac{-3}{-4} \\\\ m & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186297209 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $az=b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $z$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3pz-8 & =-z-6 \\\\ -3pz+z-8 & =-z-6+z \\\\ -3pz+z-8 & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3pz+z-8 & =-6 \\\\ -3pz+z-8+8 & =-6+8 \\\\ -3pz+z & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $z$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(-3p+1)z=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(-3p+1)}_{a}z=\\underbrace{2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-3p+1=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3p+1 & =0 \\\\ -3p+1-1 & =0-1 \\\\ -3p & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{-3p}{-3} & =\\frac{-1}{-3} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186320740 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2$. So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({z}^{2}+7z-8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+7z-8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z-8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({z}^{2}+7z-8) & =-2({(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186320743 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $3$. So, we take out a factor of $3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n3({q}^{2}-3q+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{q}^{2}-3q+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{q}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot q+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{q}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot q+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({q}^{2}-3q+5) & =3({(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\\cdot \\frac{11}{4} \\\\  & =3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{33}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186323839 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}-\\,{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=-1$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186323842 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+35\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $35$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}-\\,{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186332891 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2$. So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{3}{2}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}) & =2((x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186332892 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6$. So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x+{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x) & =6({(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}) \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186332893 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $5$. So, we take out a factor of $5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n5({x}^{2}-4x+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2}$, writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}-4x+5) & =5((x-2)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =5{(x-2)}^{2}+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5{(x-2)}^{2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186400930 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+14\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2}$, writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}-\\,{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=3$ and $q=5$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186400937 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2}$, writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}-\\,{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5)^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186477030 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+2)z-8 & =-3 \\\\ (p+2)z-8+8 & =-3+8 \\\\ \\underbrace{(p+2)}_{a}z & =\\underbrace{5}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\np+2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2 & =0 \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186477031 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(r-5)w-6 & =4 \\\\ 5(r-5)w-6+6 & =4+6 \\\\ \\underbrace{5(r-5)}_{a}w & =\\underbrace{10}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n5(r-5)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(r-5) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5(r-5)}{5} & =\\frac{0}{5} \\\\ r-5 & =0 \\\\ r & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186477034 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q+5)v+6 & =-4 \\\\ 2(q+5)v+6-6 & =-4-6 \\\\ \\underbrace{2(q+5)}_{a}v & =\\underbrace{-10}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(q+5)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q+5) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(q+5)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ q+5 & =0 \\\\ q & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186477035 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(p-3)z-8 & =2 \\\\ 7(p-3)z-8+8 & =2+8 \\\\ \\underbrace{7(p-3)}_{a}z & =\\underbrace{10}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7(p-3)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(p-3) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7(p-3)}{7} & =\\frac{0}{7} \\\\ p-3 & =0 \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186477039 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-3)w-6 & =-5 \\\\ (r-3)w-6+6 & =-5+6 \\\\ \\underbrace{(r-3)}_{a}w & =\\underbrace{1}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nr-3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r-3 & =0 \\\\ r & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186477046 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $av=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $6v$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3nv & =6v+6 \\\\ 3nv-6v & =6v+6-6v \\\\ 3nv-6v & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $v$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3n-6)v=6.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(3n-6)}_{a}v=\\underbrace{6}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3n-6=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n-6 & =0 \\\\ 3n-6+6 & =0+6 \\\\ 3n & =6 \\\\ \\frac{3n}{3} & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ n & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186477050 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $aw=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $8w$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5nw & =8w+9 \\\\ 5nw-8w & =8w+9-8w \\\\ 5nw-8w & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $w$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(5n-8)w=9.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(5n-8)}_{a}w=\\underbrace{9}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n5n-8=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n-8 & =0 \\\\ 5n-8+8 & =0+8 \\\\ 5n & =8 \\\\ \\frac{5n}{5} & =\\frac{8}{5} \\\\ n & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186477060 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $aw=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $8w$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2kw & =8w+6 \\\\ 2kw-8w & =8w+6-8w \\\\ 2kw-8w & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $w$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2k-8)w=6.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2k-8)}_{a}w=\\underbrace{6}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2k-8=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k-8 & =0 \\\\ 2k-8+8 & =0+8 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ \\frac{2k}{2} & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186477062 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $5x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7px & =5x+9 \\\\ 7px-5x & =5x+9-5x \\\\ 7px-5x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(7p-5)x=9.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(7p-5)}_{a}x=\\underbrace{9}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7p-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7p-5 & =0 \\\\ 7p-5+5 & =0+5 \\\\ 7p & =5 \\\\ \\frac{7p}{7} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186477066 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $au=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $9u$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4qu+4 & =9u-8 \\\\ 4qu-9u+4 & =9u-8-9u \\\\ 4qu-9u+4 & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4qu-9u+4 & =-8 \\\\ 4qu-9u+4-4 & =-8-4 \\\\ 4qu-9u & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $u$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(4q-9)u=-12.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(4q-9)}_{a}u=\\underbrace{-12}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4q-9=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4q-9 & =0 \\\\ 4q-9+9 & =0+9 \\\\ 4q & =9 \\\\ \\frac{4q}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ q & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186495287 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}&=(- y \\cos{(6 x )} + 3)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}&=y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )} - 6 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=- 6 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(6 \\cos{(6 x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{9}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=6 \\cos{(6 x )}$ and $Q(x)=9.$" #edb/ref 13194139535320 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186495295 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 16 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(5 x )}&=(- 4 y \\cos{(5 x )} + 3)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 16 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(5 x )}&=16 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(5 x )} - 24 y \\cos{(5 x )} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=- 24 y \\cos{(5 x )} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(24 \\cos{(5 x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{9}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=24 \\cos{(5 x )}$ and $Q(x)=9.$" #edb/ref 13194139535320 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186495297 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}&=(- y \\cos{(6 x )} + 2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}&=y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )} - 4 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=- 4 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(4 \\cos{(6 x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{4}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=4 \\cos{(6 x )}$ and $Q(x)=4.$" #edb/ref 13194139535320 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186495304 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 16 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}&=(- 4 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 3)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 16 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}&=16 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )} - 24 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=- 24 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(24 \\cos{(6 x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{9}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=24 \\cos{(6 x )}$ and $Q(x)=9.$" #edb/ref 13194139535320 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186501138 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-7$. So, we take out a factor of $-7$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-7({x}^{2}+2x-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x-1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$, writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}-{1}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+1)}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7({x}^{2}+2x-1) & =-7({(x+1)}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-7{(x+1)}^{2}+(-7)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-7{(x+1)}^{2}+14\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186501163 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $-\\frac{1}{4}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4} & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186501166 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5$. So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{4}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}) & =-5({(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186501168 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2}$, writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}-{3}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-6x+10) & =-({(x-3)}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-(x-3)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =-1-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186501188 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({t}^{2}+12t+7)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}+12t+7\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}+2\\cdot 6t+7\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $7$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{t}^{2}+2\\cdot 6t+{6}^{2}}-{6}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(t+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(t+6)^{2}-29\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7-12t-{t}^{2} & =-((t+6)^{2}-29) \\\\  & =-(t+6)^{2}+29 \\\\  & =29-(t+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186501192 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $4$. So, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n4({x}^{2}+6x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+6x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+3)}^{2}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+3)}^{2}-9.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+6x) & =4({(x+3)}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =4{(x+3)}^{2}-4\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =4{(x+3)}^{2}-36\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186501206 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-3x+1) & =-({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186501218 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3$. So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}-\\,{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2) & =-3({(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186501230 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({z}^{2}+14z+19)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+14z+19\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 7z+19\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${7}^{2}$, writing the constant term $19$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 7z+{7}^{2}}-{7}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z+7)^{2}-{7}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z+7)^{2}-30\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-19-14z-{z}^{2} & =-((z+7)^{2}-30) \\\\  & =-(z+7)^{2}+30 \\\\  & =30-(z+7)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186501236 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}+7x+11)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+11\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+11\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $11$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x-11 & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186501243 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8$. So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x) & =-8({(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186508045 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q-7)v+5 & =1 \\\\ 2(q-7)v+5-5 & =1-5 \\\\ \\underbrace{2(q-7)}_{a}v & =\\underbrace{-4}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(q-7)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q-7) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(q-7)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ q-7 & =0 \\\\ q & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186508108 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(k+3)z+8 & =1 \\\\ -7(k+3)z+8-8 & =1-8 \\\\ \\underbrace{-7(k+3)}_{a}z & =\\underbrace{-7}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-7(k+3)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(k+3) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-7(k+3)}{-7} & =\\frac{0}{-7} \\\\ k+3 & =0 \\\\ k & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186511718 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 7x-5\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${7}^{2}$, writing the constant term $-5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 7x+{7}^{2}}-\\,{7}^{2}-5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-7)^{2}-{7}^{2}-5\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-7)^{2}-54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-7$ and $q=-54$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511729 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+95\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2}$, writing the constant term $95$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}}-\\,{2}^{2}+95\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+95\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-2)^{2}+91.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-2$ and $q=91$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511732 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 6z+9\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $9$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 6z+{6}^{2}}-\\,{6}^{2}+9\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+9\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z-6)^{2}-27.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-6$ and $q=-27$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511741 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 6z+10\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 6z+{6}^{2}}-\\,{6}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z-6)^{2}-26.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511744 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x-58\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2}$, writing the constant term $-58$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}-\\,{5}^{2}-58\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}-58\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}-83.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=-83$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511753 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 9y-48\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${9}^{2}$, writing the constant term $-48$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 9y+{9}^{2}}-\\,{9}^{2}-48\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-9)^{2}-{9}^{2}-48\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-9)^{2}-129.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=9$ and $q=-129$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511762 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 5z-49\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2}$, writing the constant term $-49$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 5z+{5}^{2}}-\\,{5}^{2}-49\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}-49\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z-5)^{2}-74.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=-74$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511769 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 3z-4\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2}$, writing the constant term $-4$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 3z+{3}^{2}}-\\,{3}^{2}-4\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}-4\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z-3)^{2}-13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-3$ and $q=-13$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511786 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 10y+87\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${10}^{2}$, writing the constant term $87$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 10y+{10}^{2}}-\\,{10}^{2}+87\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+10)^{2}-{10}^{2}+87\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+10)^{2}-13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=10$ and $q=-13$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511792 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 12x+128\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${12}^{2}$, writing the constant term $128$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 12x+{12}^{2}}-\\,{12}^{2}+128\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+12)^{2}-{12}^{2}+128\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+12)^{2}-16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=12$ and $q=-16$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511794 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 6z+60\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $60$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 6z+{6}^{2}}-\\,{6}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z+6)^{2}+24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=6$ and $q=24$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511802 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 15z+208\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${15}^{2}$, writing the constant term $208$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 15z+{15}^{2}}-\\,{15}^{2}+208\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z+15)^{2}-{15}^{2}+208\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z+15)^{2}-17.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=15$ and $q=-17$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511812 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 12z+153\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${12}^{2}$, writing the constant term $153$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 12z+{12}^{2}}-\\,{12}^{2}+153\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z+12)^{2}-{12}^{2}+153\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z+12)^{2}+9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=12$ and $q=9$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511813 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 13y+176\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${13}^{2}$, writing the constant term $176$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 13y+{13}^{2}}-\\,{13}^{2}+176\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+13)^{2}-{13}^{2}+176\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+13)^{2}+7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=13$ and $q=7$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511815 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6y+59\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $59$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2}}-\\,{6}^{2}+59\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+59\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+6)^{2}+23.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=6$ and $q=23$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186511819 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 12z+138\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${12}^{2}$, writing the constant term $138$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 12z+{12}^{2}}-\\,{12}^{2}+138\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z+12)^{2}-{12}^{2}+138\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z+12)^{2}-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=12$ and $q=-6$." #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186518546 "Let $f(x)=3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4.$ We know that $(x - 2)$ and $(x - 1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x - 2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x - 2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $-14$ | $23$ | $-16$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $14$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-8$ | $7$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4}{x - 2}=3 x^{3} - 8 x^{2} + 7 x - 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4=(x - 2)(3 x^{3} - 8 x^{2} + 7 x - 2).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x - 1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3 x^{3} - 8 x^{2} + 7 x - 2).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x - 1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $-8$ | $7$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{3} - 8 x^{2} + 7 x - 2}{x - 1}=3 x^{2} - 5 x + 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4=(x - 2)(x - 1)(3 x^{2} - 5 x + 2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4=(x - 2)(x - 1)\\underbrace{(3 x - 2)(x - 1)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3 x - 2)(x - 1).$" #edb/ref 13194139535573 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518557 "Let $f(x)=x^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48.$ We know that $(x - 3)$ and $(x - 1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x - 3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x - 3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-37$ | $82$ | $-48$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $15$ | $-66$ | $48$ |\n|  | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48}{x - 3}=x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 22 x + 16,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48=(x - 3)(x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 22 x + 16).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x - 1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 22 x + 16).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x - 1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 22 x + 16}{x - 1}=x^{2} + 6 x - 16,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48=(x - 3)(x - 1)(x^{2} + 6 x - 16).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48=(x - 3)(x - 1)\\underbrace{(x - 2)(x + 8)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x - 2)(x + 8).$" #edb/ref 13194139535573 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518564 "Let $f(x)=x^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96.$ We know that $(x + 4)$ and $(x + 3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x + 3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x + 3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x + 3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $13$ | $62$ | $128$ | $96$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $-3$ | $-30$ | $-96$ | $-96$ |\n|  | $1$ | $10$ | $32$ | $32$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96}{x + 3}=x^{3} + 10 x^{2} + 32 x + 32,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96=(x + 3)(x^{3} + 10 x^{2} + 32 x + 32).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x + 4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^{3} + 10 x^{2} + 32 x + 32).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x + 4)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $10$ | $32$ | $32$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $-4$ | $-24$ | $-32$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^{3} + 10 x^{2} + 32 x + 32}{x + 4}=x^{2} + 6 x + 8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96=(x + 4)(x + 3)(x^{2} + 6 x + 8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96=(x + 4)(x + 3)\\underbrace{(x + 2)(x + 4)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x + 2)(x + 4).$" #edb/ref 13194139535573 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518575 "Let $f(x)=3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6.$ We know that $(x - 1)$ and $(x - 3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x - 1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x - 1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $-7$ | $-13$ | $23$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-17$ | $6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ | $-17$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6}{x - 1}=3 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} - 17 x + 6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6=(x - 1)(3 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} - 17 x + 6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x - 3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} - 17 x + 6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x - 3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-17$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $9$ | $15$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} - 17 x + 6}{x - 3}=3 x^{2} + 5 x - 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6=(x - 1)(x - 3)(3 x^{2} + 5 x - 2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6=(x - 1)(x - 3)\\underbrace{(3 x - 1)(x + 2)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3 x - 1)(x + 2).$" #edb/ref 13194139535573 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518576 "Let $f(x)=3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16.$ We know that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $-17$ | $16$ | $20$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $12$ | $-20$ | $-16$ | $16$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16}{x - 4}=3 x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 4 x + 4,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16=(x - 4)(3 x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 4 x + 4).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x + 1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3 x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 4 x + 4).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x + 1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $-3$ | $8$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-8$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 4 x + 4}{x + 1}=3 x^{2} - 8 x + 4,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16=(x - 4)(x + 1)(3 x^{2} - 8 x + 4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16=(x - 4)(x + 1)\\underbrace{(3 x - 2)(x - 2)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3 x - 2)(x - 2).$" #edb/ref 13194139535573 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518908 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =\\left|\\begin{matrix}2 v^{3}&6 u v^{2}\\\\6 u&2 v\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =(2 v^{3})\\cdot (2 v)-(6 u v^{2})\\cdot (6 u)\\\\& =- 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 4 v^{4}\\\\& =- 4 v^{2} \\left(3 u - v\\right) \\left(3 u + v\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(-1,2)} & =\\left(- 4 v^{2} \\left(3 u - v\\right) \\left(3 u + v\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=-1,v=2}\\\\& =- 4 (2)^{2} \\left(3 (-1) - (2)\\right) \\left(3 (-1) + (2)\\right)\\\\& =-80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|-80|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =80\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535581 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518915 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =\\left|\\begin{matrix}3 v^{2}&6 u v\\\\2 u&4 v\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =(3 v^{2})\\cdot (4 v)-(6 u v)\\cdot (2 u)\\\\& =- 12 u^{2} v + 12 v^{3}\\\\& =- 12 v \\left(u - v\\right) \\left(u + v\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(-3,-1)} & =\\left(- 12 v \\left(u - v\\right) \\left(u + v\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=-3,v=-1}\\\\& =- 12 (-1) \\left(- (-1) + (-3)\\right) \\left((-1) + (-3)\\right)\\\\& =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|96|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =96\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535581 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518926 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =\\left|\\begin{matrix}4 \\left(u + 2 v\\right)&8 \\left(u + 2 v\\right)\\\\3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2}&- 3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =(4 \\left(u + 2 v\\right))\\cdot (- 3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2})-(8 \\left(u + 2 v\\right))\\cdot (3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2})\\\\& =- 36 u^{3} + 108 u v^{2} - 72 v^{3}\\\\& =- 36 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2} \\left(u + 2 v\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(1,2)} & =\\left(- 36 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2} \\left(u + 2 v\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=1,v=2}\\\\& =- 36 \\left((1) - (2)\\right)^{2} \\left((1) + 2 (2)\\right)\\\\& =-180\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|-180|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =180\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535581 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518930 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =\\left|\\begin{matrix}3 v^{3}&9 u v^{2}\\\\2 u&4 v\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =(3 v^{3})\\cdot (4 v)-(9 u v^{2})\\cdot (2 u)\\\\& =- 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 12 v^{4}\\\\& =- 6 v^{2} \\left(3 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(2,-1)} & =\\left(- 6 v^{2} \\left(3 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=2,v=-1}\\\\& =- 6 (-1)^{2} \\left(- 2 (-1)^{2} + 3 (2)^{2}\\right)\\\\& =-60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|-60|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =60\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535581 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518944 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =\\left|\\begin{matrix}3 \\left(u + 2 v\\right)^{2}&6 \\left(u + 2 v\\right)^{2}\\\\2 \\left(u - v\\right)&- 2 \\left(u - v\\right)\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =(3 \\left(u + 2 v\\right)^{2})\\cdot (- 2 \\left(u - v\\right))-(6 \\left(u + 2 v\\right)^{2})\\cdot (2 \\left(u - v\\right))\\\\& =- 18 u^{3} - 54 u^{2} v + 72 v^{3}\\\\& =- 18 \\left(u - v\\right) \\left(u + 2 v\\right)^{2}\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(-1,2)} & =\\left(- 18 \\left(u - v\\right) \\left(u + 2 v\\right)^{2}\\right)\\big|_{u=-1,v=2}\\\\& =- 18 \\left((-1) - (2)\\right) \\left((-1) + 2 (2)\\right)^{2}\\\\& =486\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|486|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =486\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535581 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518946 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =\\left|\\begin{matrix}6 \\left(u + v\\right)^{2}&6 \\left(u + v\\right)^{2}\\\\4 \\left(2 u - v\\right)&- 2 \\left(2 u - v\\right)\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =(6 \\left(u + v\\right)^{2})\\cdot (- 2 \\left(2 u - v\\right))-(6 \\left(u + v\\right)^{2})\\cdot (4 \\left(2 u - v\\right))\\\\& =- 72 u^{3} - 108 u^{2} v + 36 v^{3}\\\\& =- 36 \\left(u + v\\right)^{2} \\left(2 u - v\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(-2,1)} & =\\left(- 36 \\left(u + v\\right)^{2} \\left(2 u - v\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=-2,v=1}\\\\& =- 36 \\left((-2) + (1)\\right)^{2} \\left(2 (-2) - (1)\\right)\\\\& =180\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|180|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =180\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535581 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518954 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =\\left|\\begin{matrix}8 \\left(2 u + v\\right)&4 \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\\\3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2}&- 3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =(8 \\left(2 u + v\\right))\\cdot (- 3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2})-(4 \\left(2 u + v\\right))\\cdot (3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2})\\\\& =- 72 u^{3} + 108 u^{2} v - 36 v^{3}\\\\& =- 36 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2} \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(-2,-1)} & =\\left(- 36 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2} \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=-2,v=-1}\\\\& =- 36 \\left(- (-1) + (-2)\\right)^{2} \\left((-1) + 2 (-2)\\right)\\\\& =180\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|180|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =180\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535581 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518955 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =\\left|\\begin{matrix}2 v^{3}&6 u v^{2}\\\\2 u&2 v\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =(2 v^{3})\\cdot (2 v)-(6 u v^{2})\\cdot (2 u)\\\\& =- 12 u^{2} v^{2} + 4 v^{4}\\\\& =- 4 v^{2} \\left(3 u^{2} - v^{2}\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(-2,-1)} & =\\left(- 4 v^{2} \\left(3 u^{2} - v^{2}\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=-2,v=-1}\\\\& =- 4 (-1)^{2} \\left(- (-1)^{2} + 3 (-2)^{2}\\right)\\\\& =-44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|-44|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =44\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535581 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518958 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =\\left|\\begin{matrix}3 v^{3}&9 u v^{2}\\\\2 u&4 v\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =(3 v^{3})\\cdot (4 v)-(9 u v^{2})\\cdot (2 u)\\\\& =- 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 12 v^{4}\\\\& =- 6 v^{2} \\left(3 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(3,1)} & =\\left(- 6 v^{2} \\left(3 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=3,v=1}\\\\& =- 6 (1)^{2} \\left(- 2 (1)^{2} + 3 (3)^{2}\\right)\\\\& =-150\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|-150|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =150\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535581 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186518966 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =\\left|\\begin{matrix}8 \\left(2 u + v\\right)&4 \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\\\3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2}&- 3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =(8 \\left(2 u + v\\right))\\cdot (- 3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2})-(4 \\left(2 u + v\\right))\\cdot (3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2})\\\\& =- 72 u^{3} + 108 u^{2} v - 36 v^{3}\\\\& =- 36 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2} \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(1,-1)} & =\\left(- 36 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2} \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=1,v=-1}\\\\& =- 36 \\left(- (-1) + (1)\\right)^{2} \\left((-1) + 2 (1)\\right)\\\\& =-144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|-144|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =144\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535581 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186521467 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8$. So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot z+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{16}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16}) & =-8({(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186521522 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+z+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}z+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}z+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({z}^{2}+z+2) & =-({(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186521557 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2$. So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({x}^{2}+3x-2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+3x-2) & =-2({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{17}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186521565 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}+3y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+3y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}-3y-5 & =-({(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}-{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186530765 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2 y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,0\\rangle, \\\\ r(\\pi) & =\\langle \\pi,\\pi^{2}\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi,\\pi^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi,\\pi^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\left(\\pi^{2}\\right)\\right)^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\pi\\right) \\right)}- 2 e^{\\left(\\pi^{2}\\right)}\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}- 2 e^{\\left(0\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0- 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}\\right)-\\left(0-2\\right) \\\\  & =2 - 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535827 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186532669 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+26\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2}$, writing the constant term $26$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}-\\,{4}^{2}+26\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+26\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}+10.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186532681 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 6y+15\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $15$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2}}-\\,{6}^{2}+15\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+15\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-6)^{2}-21.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186532698 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 10y+106\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${10}^{2}$, writing the constant term $106$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 10y+{10}^{2}}-\\,{10}^{2}+106\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+10)^{2}-{10}^{2}+106\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+10)^{2}+6.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186566227 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y+21)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y+21\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+21\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $21$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y-21 & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186566232 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $10$. So, we take out a factor of $10$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{100}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}}-\\,{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}) & =10({(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}) \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+10\\cdot \\frac{3}{50} \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186566240 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $4$. So, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n4({x}^{2}+8x+10)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2}$, writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}}-{4}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-6\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+8x+10) & =4((x+4)^{2}-6) \\\\  & =4{(x+4)}^{2}-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =4{(x+4)}^{2}-24\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186566242 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-12x+35\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+35\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $35$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-12x+35) & =-({(x-6)}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-{(x-6)}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-(x-6)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186566243 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2$. So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({x}^{2}+3x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+3x) & =-2({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186566256 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $7$. So, we take out a factor of $7$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n7({x}^{2}-3x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7({x}^{2}-3x) & =7({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-7\\cdot \\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{63}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186566260 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-8y+60\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+60\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2}$, writing the constant term $60$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}-{4}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}+44.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-8y+60) & =-({(y-4)}^{2}+44) \\\\  & =-{(y-4)}^{2}-44 \\\\  & =-44-(y-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186566264 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-4$. So, we take out a factor of $-4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-4({x}^{2}-2x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-1)}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}^{2}-2x) & =-4({(x-1)}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-4{(x-1)}^{2}+(-4)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4{(x-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186566268 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({a}^{2}+10a-16)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+10a-16\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+2\\cdot 5a-16\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${5}^{2}$, writing the constant term $16$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{a}^{2}+2\\cdot 5a+{5}^{2}}-{5}^{2}-16\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(a+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}-16\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(a+5)^{2}-41\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-10a-{a}^{2} & =-((a+5)^{2}-41) \\\\  & =-(a+5)^{2}+41 \\\\  & =41-(a+5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536886 #edb/ref 17592186576411]}
